I live in Salt Lake and yeah, there's like this annual offsite where the whole company sounds like basically just, you know, has a party for a week.

30
00:02:14,080 --> 00:02:18,800
And so, you know, I feel very good at coming in as my first day being part of this party.

31
00:02:19,360 --> 00:02:20,720
So, yeah, it's great timing.

32
00:02:20,880 --> 00:02:21,680
I'm looking forward to it.

33
00:02:22,000 --> 00:02:23,040
Yeah, it's it's funny.

34
00:02:23,360 --> 00:02:26,080
My first day was actually at a company.any offsite as well.

35
00:02:26,480 --> 00:02:30,960
And similarly, right before I started, you've been on my list.

36
00:02:31,520 --> 00:02:36,320
Honestly, since Stuff came out, Aggressively Delightful is almost an understatement for this app.

37
00:02:36,640 --> 00:02:41,680
What I always do before we get into the show now is I have you give a quick elevator pitch for the app.

38
00:02:42,000 --> 00:02:46,240
This is a task management app, so it's a little clearer to describe probably than some of the other ones we've had.

39
00:02:46,480 --> 00:02:49,600
Yeah, we're not really breaking new ground necessarily with task management.

40
00:02:50,000 --> 00:02:53,440
It's a concept that's tried and true, but I really like Aggressively Delightful.

41
00:02:53,680 --> 00:02:54,960
So I'm going to use that more often.

42
00:02:55,600 --> 00:02:58,800
As far as elevator pitch goes, I've always said stuff is an app.

43
00:02:59,040 --> 00:03:00,480
It's a to-do list app that.

44
00:03:00,640 --> 00:03:19,760
That is simple enough that my grandma can pick it up and add some tasks and check them off, but it is powerful enough that someone like me with thousands of tasks can go in there and set up task dependencies across multiple lists, all organized by tag and have my shared list with different teams.

45
00:03:20,400 --> 00:03:34,480
So it tries to hit that middle ground between being simple and easy to pick up and start using, but also very powerful and can kind of grow with you depending on how much you want to rely on it.

46
00:03:34,960 --> 00:03:35,280
Perfect.

47
00:03:36,160 --> 00:03:41,040
Before we get into the story of stuff, I want to introduce everybody to who you are.

48
00:03:41,200 --> 00:03:45,760
So the three questions I always ask to kick off that conversation is, where are you from?

49
00:03:46,240 --> 00:03:49,040
Do you have a formal education related to what you do?

50
00:03:49,520 --> 00:03:54,240
And then we can talk about what your career was like leading into you making stuff.

51
00:03:54,800 --> 00:03:54,800
Yeah.

52
00:03:55,280 --> 00:03:59,920
So I'm based in Utah, right around Salt Lake City, like we mentioned.

53
00:04:00,480 --> 00:04:01,360
I've grown up here.

54
00:04:01,600 --> 00:04:08,640
I've lived a couple other places throughout my short time here, but mostly in Utah.

55
00:04:09,360 --> 00:04:12,800
I don't have a formal education related to coding.

56
00:04:13,680 --> 00:04:16,880
My education kind of takes a twisted path, so we could get into that.

57
00:04:17,440 --> 00:04:18,960
But do you not have a formal education?

58
00:04:19,040 --> 00:04:19,520
Oh, yeah.

59
00:04:19,680 --> 00:04:20,480
I'm curious about that.

60
00:04:20,720 --> 00:04:35,360
That's why I asked that question is I love that with this field of indie development, there's plenty of people that have computer science backgrounds, but there's people from all sorts of different backgrounds and it informs so much of what you end up creating down the line.

61
00:04:35,600 --> 00:04:37,040
And so, yeah, I love that stuff.

62
00:04:37,600 --> 00:04:37,680
Yeah.

63
00:04:38,000 --> 00:04:48,240
I mean, the funny thing is like with this new position that I'm taking at RevenueCat that I'm really excited about as a developer advocate, it kind of brings together everything that I've done in the past.

64
00:04:48,800 --> 00:04:51,440
But it's felt disconnected up until this point essentially.

65
00:04:52,160 --> 00:04:59,040
I went to school at Brigham Young University, BYU, and was first enrolled as a film major.

66
00:04:59,360 --> 00:05:00,880
So I really wanted to be a director.

67
00:05:01,120 --> 00:05:01,520
Oh man.

68
00:05:01,840 --> 00:05:11,120
And it's just crazy how many people their background is like film or visual effects or something in film making or, you know, video.

69
00:05:11,840 --> 00:05:12,160
Totally.

70
00:05:12,320 --> 00:05:12,400
Yeah.

71
00:05:12,640 --> 00:05:16,160
It's such a cool space and it's such a creative space.

72
00:05:16,320 --> 00:05:20,880
And I just feel like the stage is wide open for people to do really cool things in that space.

73
00:05:20,960 --> 00:05:22,160
So that was what excited me about it.

74
00:05:22,320 --> 00:05:26,720
Obviously, it's all a whole different story.

75
00:05:26,960 --> 00:05:31,520
So yeah, I started out and kind of really wanted to make movies and direct movies.

76
00:05:31,760 --> 00:05:34,880
So I had a bunch of scripts that I was writing and putting some stuff together.

77
00:05:35,360 --> 00:05:46,240
But about halfway through that process, I attended a little session with my roommate for the advertising program that they were putting together.

78
00:05:46,640 --> 00:05:50,480
And the reason my roommate invited me is because I've always been this really big Apple fanboy.

79
00:05:50,880 --> 00:05:58,000
And the session was being hosted by the head of all Apple advertising, who is a BYU grad.

80
00:05:58,400 --> 00:05:59,680
So I was invited for that reason.

81
00:06:00,720 --> 00:06:11,920
Essentially, that one hour that I spent in that meeting was so influential for me that I decided I would drop all my filmmaking aspirations and join the advertising program.

82
00:06:12,560 --> 00:06:18,800
I also saw it as my path to working at Apple, which is kind of what I've always wanted to do as it was my dream company.

83
00:06:19,200 --> 00:06:22,320
Apple was your dream company even whenever you were getting a film degree.

84
00:06:22,640 --> 00:06:28,480
Yeah, I've I'm the kind of kid so I grew up watching Steve Jobs on our iMac in the kitchen.

85
00:06:29,040 --> 00:06:42,800
Like while my family was like eating dinner, I would come home from school, throw in my headphones and watch the keynote that had happened while I was at school and like full on like clapping with the audience and like cheering and everything with the new products.

86
00:06:43,120 --> 00:07:02,000
And so my family's just trying to have a nice little dinner and I'm like just yelling at the computer, but I've always been a huge Apple fanboy and like I just really have loved I cried when I got my first iPod for Christmas and I just like love everything about Apple, the brand, the mission, the emphasis on design.

87
00:07:02,640 --> 00:07:04,400
So it's been like a huge driving force.

88
00:07:04,720 --> 00:07:08,720
I mean, even when I was doing film stuff, like, you know, I was really into all of that.

89
00:07:09,200 --> 00:07:16,160
So anyways, yeah, I joined the advertising program and thought, you know, hey, I'm going to make awesome ads for Apple because I believe in this company.

90
00:07:16,400 --> 00:07:23,280
And while I was in the advertising program, I was using a bunch of different apps to try and manage my different projects.

91
00:07:23,680 --> 00:07:25,520
I'm also like a huge productivity nerd.

92
00:07:25,600 --> 00:07:32,400
So the other thing that I did on our family iMac was downloading like all the productivity apps as soon as the Mac App Store came out, because that was like a huge deal for me.

93
00:07:32,560 --> 00:07:34,800
Like that was a big launch, that Mac App Store.

94
00:07:35,200 --> 00:07:36,400
And so you're the one.

95
00:07:36,640 --> 00:07:36,720
Yes.

96
00:07:37,440 --> 00:07:37,520
Yeah.

97
00:07:37,760 --> 00:07:40,240
So I was the one using that app when that came out.

98
00:07:40,640 --> 00:07:42,720
And yeah, so like a huge productivity nerd.

99
00:07:42,880 --> 00:07:48,320
So anyway, I was really heavy into Evernote at the time and I was sending Evernote all these feature requests.

100
00:07:49,120 --> 00:07:59,600
Just like, hey, like, you know, this is how this should be designed because like it would make more sense if this information was here and I want this type of view or like to be able to filter by this tag and this notebook.

101
00:08:00,160 --> 00:08:06,800
So I sent them probably over thirty feature requests over the course of a year or so and I never heard back.

102
00:08:07,600 --> 00:08:13,920
And so I was just this college kid just like trying to give free advice and apparently they didn't want it.

103
00:08:14,560 --> 00:08:23,920
So I decided that I would just do it myself and that kind of kicked off my journey of learning to code at WWDC that I was watching.

104
00:08:24,800 --> 00:08:26,000
They announced some new tool.

105
00:08:26,240 --> 00:08:28,000
It was the year before SwiftUI came out.

106
00:08:28,240 --> 00:08:29,440
I was like, yeah, I can do this.

107
00:08:29,760 --> 00:08:33,840
So I started learning to code and started making a note taking app to compete with Evernote.

108
00:08:34,160 --> 00:08:36,320
And this was while you were in college?

109
00:08:36,800 --> 00:08:39,280
This was while I was in college, yeah, as a advertising major.

110
00:08:39,760 --> 00:08:47,920
Okay, so you're a advertising major and you just started building your own app then, was that what became stuff or was that a separate app that came out earlier?

111
00:08:48,320 --> 00:08:49,680
Yeah, so that was a note taking app.

112
00:08:49,920 --> 00:09:00,480
I tweeted out to the CEO of Evernote at the time, who was Ian Small, and I just said, hey, you've got a great product, but like, I'm coming for you guys and I'm going to knock Evernote off the cliff.

113
00:09:01,040 --> 00:09:04,240
And he responded, I think he just said like, okay, that, that, that.

114
00:09:04,560 --> 00:09:08,160
He immediately sold the bending spoons and that's when everything, you know, fell down.

115
00:09:08,720 --> 00:09:09,760
You took down Evernote.

116
00:09:10,640 --> 00:09:14,400
So I feel like, yeah, I feel a lot of, you know, responsibility for that fall.

117
00:09:15,200 --> 00:09:25,440
But yeah, I mean, I was very ambitious about what was possible with coding and, you know, how easy it would be to make an Evernote competitor that was making as much as Evernote was making at the time.

118
00:09:25,920 --> 00:09:29,280
Quickly learned that, you know, coding is much more difficult than I anticipated.

119
00:09:29,920 --> 00:09:34,720
And, uh, but I did launch my note taking app, which was called Mighty Note.

120
00:09:34,960 --> 00:09:36,240
And Mighty Note did.

121
00:09:37,360 --> 00:09:40,240
It didn't do too bad for my standards at least as like a college student.

122
00:09:40,560 --> 00:09:48,000
It did get me into WWDC the next year in terms of I applied and took it there to show it to some Apple engineers.

123
00:09:48,560 --> 00:09:51,520
I wasn't like a student scholar or anything like that, but I at least got some.

124
00:09:52,000 --> 00:09:52,960
You got the golden ticket.

125
00:09:53,600 --> 00:09:54,160
Yeah, right.

126
00:09:54,400 --> 00:09:57,280
And I got some like interest from Apple engineers, which was cool.

127
00:09:57,680 --> 00:10:00,960
So I soon there after SwiftUI was announced at WWDC.

128
00:10:01,120 --> 00:10:05,040
And so I kind of wanted to see the capabilities of that.

129
00:10:05,360 --> 00:10:10,880
And simultaneously there was a university competition for the best app of the year kind of thing.

130
00:10:11,360 --> 00:10:17,120
And so I took SwiftUI and I took that university competition and I made an app called Achievements, which was a habit tracking app.

131
00:10:17,440 --> 00:10:22,080
So again, like in that productivity space, and won the competition of Best App of the Year.

132
00:10:22,560 --> 00:10:23,840
And so I was very happy to see it.

133
00:10:23,920 --> 00:10:25,200
And you released it to the store too?

134
00:10:25,600 --> 00:10:26,080
I did, yeah.

135
00:10:26,160 --> 00:10:27,200
I released it to the store.

136
00:10:27,680 --> 00:10:36,080
I got some cool interactions with Paul Hudson who helped me get over fifty thousand downloads just by, like, as part of this competition.

137
00:10:36,240 --> 00:10:37,280
So he put it out there.

138
00:10:37,680 --> 00:10:38,720
So all that was really helpful.

139
00:10:38,800 --> 00:10:43,520
And then the competition got me some funding to kind of decide where I would go next with the company.

140
00:10:43,920 --> 00:10:45,600
Both of these apps, were they paid?

141
00:10:45,920 --> 00:10:46,880
Were they totally free?

142
00:10:47,280 --> 00:10:48,560
Like, what was the kind of business model?

143
00:10:49,280 --> 00:10:51,040
Mighty Note was free with the subscription.

144
00:10:51,440 --> 00:11:00,880
Achievements started as an upfront purchase of five dollars, which was good at first because that was featured by nine to five Mac in an article written up by Chance Miller.

145
00:11:01,520 --> 00:11:04,640
And so that got me a lot of good downloads up at first.

146
00:11:04,800 --> 00:11:08,160
And then I ended up switching Achievements to a subscription model.

147
00:11:08,880 --> 00:11:12,160
So free upfront and then just gave everyone who had purchased it lifetime access.

148
00:11:12,640 --> 00:11:15,920
And how did that go for, I guess, any of those apps?

149
00:11:16,160 --> 00:11:21,200
Was it kind of, I mean, you got press right from the bat, but like, were they immediately making money?

150
00:11:21,600 --> 00:11:24,560
Did they grow into a kind of a business, you know, capital B business?

151
00:11:25,120 --> 00:11:25,920
early on or ever?

152
00:11:26,560 --> 00:11:31,520
Yeah, I neither Capital B business nor lowercase B business to be honest.

153
00:11:32,080 --> 00:11:43,600
They made pretty, pretty chum change, uh, which I was excited about at the time because I thought it was cool that, you know, people around the world were interested in something that I was doing, but nothing to the point where I could quit my job or anything like that.

154
00:11:44,000 --> 00:11:48,480
So yeah, it was definitely like still a work in progress, but I at least kind of saw the potential there.

155
00:11:48,800 --> 00:11:49,840
What did you attribute that to?

156
00:11:49,920 --> 00:11:52,640
Because you said fifty thousand downloads on achievements.

157
00:11:53,200 --> 00:11:56,720
So like there was at least a lot of initial interest.

158
00:11:57,200 --> 00:12:04,560
Did people churn pretty quick or were they continuing to ch use it but they just didn't, you know, bump up to the paid tier?

159
00:12:04,640 --> 00:12:10,480
Or like what do you kind of attribute to the fact that it wasn't making money even though you at least had a decent top of funnel?

160
00:12:11,040 --> 00:12:28,960
Yeah, I think that something that I've had to figure out with apps and like I think that all app developers have to figure this out, I think that I'm pretty good at making flashy posts on social media where you show the features accurately and have screens that look really, really nice.

161
00:12:29,520 --> 00:12:33,520
But the thing that I've been learning about is optimizing onboarding experience.

162
00:12:33,920 --> 00:12:35,920
Your app store page is a huge deal.

163
00:12:36,720 --> 00:12:40,640
in terms of impressions on your App Store page versus people that actually download the app.

164
00:12:41,440 --> 00:12:45,600
And then, yeah, like having a really great upsell for your paywall.

165
00:12:46,240 --> 00:12:55,600
So emphasizing features at contextual moments when they're relevant and also having features that are worth upgrading for.

166
00:12:56,320 --> 00:13:08,880
I think that with like achievements, for example, I probably drew the line too close in terms of I didn't let people do enough with the app before allowing them or like prompting them to subscribe.

167
00:13:08,960 --> 00:13:12,560
So they didn't feel the value before they made the choice not to.

168
00:13:12,960 --> 00:13:13,680
100%.

169
00:13:14,080 --> 00:13:14,160
Yeah.

170
00:13:14,480 --> 00:13:21,360
And so I might lean a little too far the other direction now with stuff where we have way more users than we have paid users.

171
00:13:21,680 --> 00:13:23,360
And I'm pretty okay with that right now.

172
00:13:24,080 --> 00:13:26,400
But yeah, it's kind of a hard line to find.

173
00:13:26,560 --> 00:13:37,280
You have to find a balance of, you know, letting people use the app and understand the value of it and also not feel like they're going to be kicked out or reach some sort of arbitrary limit before they can really get into it.

174
00:13:37,680 --> 00:13:41,040
But also having paid features that are worth upgrading for.

175
00:13:41,680 --> 00:13:45,280
So these were apps that you made while you were in school.

176
00:13:45,920 --> 00:13:48,000
So once you graduated, where did you go there?

177
00:13:48,000 --> 00:13:49,600
Did you go like, this is it.

178
00:13:49,760 --> 00:13:53,040
I'm going to be an indie developer and this is this is my sort of career?

179
00:13:53,680 --> 00:13:53,760
Yeah.

180
00:13:54,080 --> 00:13:56,960
So after achievements, I actually started stuff.

181
00:13:57,120 --> 00:14:02,800
I started working on stuff because I had this idea of this task manager and it was inspired actually by Wonderlist back in the day.

182
00:14:02,960 --> 00:14:04,160
I don't know if that means anything to you.

183
00:14:04,480 --> 00:14:04,800
That was my app.

184
00:14:05,120 --> 00:14:05,520
That was my app.

185
00:14:06,080 --> 00:14:08,240
Wonderlist because I was on the Android.

186
00:14:08,800 --> 00:14:11,360
Wonderlist was cross platform and I loved Wonderlist.

187
00:14:11,440 --> 00:14:11,520
Yeah.

188
00:14:11,760 --> 00:14:12,880
I don't remember what happened to it.

189
00:14:13,040 --> 00:14:15,040
It got acquired by Microsoft, right?

190
00:14:15,520 --> 00:14:15,600
Yeah.

191
00:14:15,840 --> 00:14:17,040
It became Microsoft Todo.

192
00:14:17,280 --> 00:14:17,520
Todo.

193
00:14:17,760 --> 00:14:17,840
Yeah.

194
00:14:18,000 --> 00:14:18,320
That's right.

195
00:14:18,480 --> 00:14:18,800
That's right.

196
00:14:19,040 --> 00:14:19,120
Okay.

197
00:14:19,440 --> 00:14:27,360
And I don't remember Todo being bad, but I bounced in during that transition for some reason, I don't remember why, but yeah, I loved Wonderlist.

198
00:14:27,680 --> 00:14:30,160
Wonderlist was the real deal.

199
00:14:30,560 --> 00:14:30,640
Yeah.

200
00:14:31,040 --> 00:14:37,760
Oh man, I still have screenshots from back in my old iPod touch days of like my Wonderlist lists getting completed.

201
00:14:38,320 --> 00:14:45,600
And so yeah, Wonderlist was really like my app, like it got me through high school and I just loved everything about it.

202
00:14:45,840 --> 00:14:53,520
And so stuff really sprouted out of like, you know, building something to kind of bring back that feeling that I had with Wonderlist around that.

203
00:14:53,760 --> 00:14:54,880
So I started working on stuff.

204
00:14:55,120 --> 00:14:59,280
I also quit my job just on a whim because not that I was making enough money.

205
00:15:00,000 --> 00:15:02,800
But I was really scared about quitting my job.

206
00:15:02,960 --> 00:15:05,440
And so I thought, ah, if that's the only reason then I should probably do it.

207
00:15:05,680 --> 00:15:07,840
This was I quit my job after college?

208
00:15:08,160 --> 00:15:09,600
This was in my last year of college.

209
00:15:10,560 --> 00:15:11,840
Okay, so your job I still don't know.

