00:46:49,120 --> 00:47:09,920
Yeah, it feels more like a retention kind of thing or driving people to which I guess is all related to retention, but driving people towards using new features outside just the design of the app itself, especially if it sounds like one of your core ideas with the whole it's simple to use, but if you need it, these things are there is like progressive.

598
00:47:10,480 --> 00:47:11,520
Is it progressive disclosure?

599
00:47:11,760 --> 00:47:11,840
No.

600
00:47:12,000 --> 00:47:12,640
What is that called?

601
00:47:13,120 --> 00:47:15,520
What is the is it progressive disclosure?

602
00:47:15,600 --> 00:47:16,080
Is that the right?

603
00:47:16,240 --> 00:47:17,440
Now I'm I'm forgetting the name.

604
00:47:17,600 --> 00:47:22,480
The, you know, where you you only reveal features to people at the point where they need them.

605
00:47:22,640 --> 00:47:23,840
Otherwise it's sort of sure.

606
00:47:24,000 --> 00:47:24,000
Yeah.

607
00:47:24,880 --> 00:47:25,360
hidden away.

608
00:47:25,680 --> 00:47:29,120
The positive to that mechanism is that it makes an app that's a lot more approachable.

609
00:47:29,520 --> 00:47:32,960
The negative is like a lot of Apple software, you don't even know it's there.

610
00:47:33,200 --> 00:47:34,880
So you never even find it.

611
00:47:35,200 --> 00:47:48,240
And so doing like product updates like that for your more hardcore users who kind of keep up with you all the time is a good way to drive more people that try out new features, which again, all of that sort of feeds more into retention.

612
00:47:48,480 --> 00:47:55,040
Like you're a lot less likely to leave if you're utilizing more and more of the features of the app and you come to rely on them.

613
00:47:55,440 --> 00:47:56,640
Yeah, I totally agree.

614
00:47:56,960 --> 00:48:07,280
And especially for people who don't opt for the lifetime purchase and are paying a subscription, I think that they deserve to know what their ongoing cost is going to.

615
00:48:08,080 --> 00:48:14,080
So there are always server costs, right, that I'm paying for, which is part of the reason why the lifetime cost is so high.

616
00:48:15,120 --> 00:48:21,200
But there's also just, you know, I if you're subscribed to something, you want to see new content coming to it.

617
00:48:21,840 --> 00:48:30,480
And so stuff is in very active development and I want to make sure that people know that and feel good about, you know, where they're putting their tasks.

618
00:48:30,800 --> 00:48:42,480
That's the other thing that's, you know, kind of difficult with a lot of apps, but productivity, especially I feel this where you are hesitant about who you trust with your long-term investment.

619
00:48:42,880 --> 00:48:51,040
And so I love seeing my tasks back several years and, you know, seeing what I did back in 2023, what I checked off.

620
00:48:51,200 --> 00:48:58,480
And like, I'm making this cool new feature of like a graph that like shows where you spent most of your time in like different categories.

621
00:48:59,280 --> 00:49:00,880
And so it's cool to see that.

622
00:49:01,440 --> 00:49:10,240
But yeah, I mean, if an app is going to shut down in a couple of years or, you know, stop getting developed, it makes you, you know, obviously not want to invest in that.

623
00:49:10,800 --> 00:49:18,800
And so that's another thing that I like the dev logs is it shows that I'm committed to the project and I still really enjoy it and will for a long time.

624
00:49:19,520 --> 00:49:26,480
And that it's not just a random app that you stumbled over in the app store that who really knows what's happening with it.

625
00:49:27,040 --> 00:49:35,600
Especially with something as important as your reminders or your notes or these kind of core pieces of your digital life, I guess.

626
00:49:36,160 --> 00:49:37,200
Or photos, obviously.

627
00:49:37,760 --> 00:49:38,480
Yeah, photos.

628
00:49:38,720 --> 00:49:39,600
Photos are a big one.

629
00:49:39,840 --> 00:49:42,240
But yeah, I mean, people are very attached to their tutor lists.

630
00:49:42,640 --> 00:49:43,440
And I'm one of those people.

631
00:49:43,600 --> 00:49:45,840
And so I understand that feeling a lot.

632
00:49:46,960 --> 00:49:51,840
And you don't want to just, you know, platform hop all the time because you kind of lose some of that traction.

633
00:49:52,000 --> 00:50:01,680
I mean, I've got tasks and stuff that are planned out ten years from now, like renewing my driver's license or even things on my bucket list that I just want to get to eventually.

634
00:50:02,240 --> 00:50:06,320
And so changing to do list apps would mean setting all that up again, making sure I didn't miss anything.

635
00:50:07,440 --> 00:50:10,320
And so, yeah, I want to be able to trust something for a long period of time.

636
00:50:10,480 --> 00:50:11,680
And so stuff is that thing.

637
00:50:12,160 --> 00:50:15,680
And then you already mentioned it, but just recently you released the Mac version of the app.

638
00:50:16,240 --> 00:50:19,920
So you had already mentioned that that was one of the things people were requesting from day one.

639
00:50:20,480 --> 00:50:23,680
What was the process like bringing that over to the Mac?

640
00:50:24,000 --> 00:50:26,880
This was your first major Mac app, right?

641
00:50:27,360 --> 00:50:29,120
Yeah, I've never done a Mac app before.

642
00:50:29,760 --> 00:50:38,880
And so it all started with me buying Hacking with Swift macOS edition and buying Paul Hudson's book on like how to make a Mac app.

643
00:50:39,600 --> 00:50:47,520
But it quickly changed to being driven pretty heavily by Codex and Cloud and kind of letting all of that do the development.

644
00:50:48,080 --> 00:50:52,160
I'm still somebody that's pretty hands on in terms of like vibe coding.

645
00:50:52,960 --> 00:51:04,240
Like I'll still I've heard that this is archaic now, but like I make plans for everything and then like pass them between the agents and have them review each other's plans and then I review all of the code before it goes in.

646
00:51:05,440 --> 00:51:18,800
But just the amount of things that I didn't have to spend hours learning how to how do I do this on the Mac and just letting that, you know, kind of give me a first-pass prototype saved me a ton of time and honestly let the app come out sooner than it would have.

647
00:51:19,600 --> 00:51:20,480
That's really awesome.

648
00:51:20,720 --> 00:51:37,280
Were there were there any interesting things you had to do differently in particular when it comes to like design of the app, not just visually but like how it works as we said that were very different than especially the iPad, even though they're both larger screen dev devices?

649
00:51:37,760 --> 00:51:40,960
Yeah, the iPad is becoming more and more like the Mac, which is really nice.

650
00:51:41,120 --> 00:51:45,120
Like the menu bar, for example, is available across both.

651
00:51:45,840 --> 00:51:52,480
But there were a lot of different features that I wanted to have on Mac that people just kind of expect to be there, even if you don't think about it.

652
00:51:52,960 --> 00:51:57,520
Like undo, for example, check a task complete and being able to undo it with Command Z.

653
00:51:58,000 --> 00:52:00,000
Like that's something that you just expect to work.

654
00:52:00,240 --> 00:52:05,040
And if it doesn't work, it's more surprising than anything and it could cause people to bounce.

655
00:52:05,360 --> 00:52:11,840
Are you saying that people don't by default shake their phone violently to undo on the iPhone just as often as the Mac?

656
00:52:12,640 --> 00:52:14,240
Exactly what I'm saying, Charlie.

657
00:52:16,560 --> 00:52:19,760
I forget that undo exists on the iPhone, if I'm being honest.

658
00:52:20,480 --> 00:52:26,000
So yeah, I mean, there were a lot of things like that where it's like, it's just not a thing on iPhone, but like on Mac, it has to be a thing.

659
00:52:26,640 --> 00:52:34,320
Multi Windows support was another one where I didn't really support that on iPad just because how many people are managing multiple windows on their iPad.

660
00:52:34,720 --> 00:52:38,320
It causes more confusion than I feel like it helps on the iPad.

661
00:52:38,640 --> 00:52:39,280
100%.

662
00:52:39,440 --> 00:52:40,400
And it's also very buggy.

663
00:52:40,480 --> 00:52:41,280
That's a different discussion.

664
00:52:41,520 --> 00:52:41,600
Yeah.

665
00:52:41,840 --> 00:52:41,920
Yeah.

666
00:52:42,160 --> 00:52:42,240
Yeah.

667
00:52:42,720 --> 00:52:43,840
It needs some word.

668
00:52:44,400 --> 00:52:44,480
Yeah.

669
00:52:44,720 --> 00:52:45,680
So there were things like that.

670
00:52:46,400 --> 00:52:50,640
But I mean, design wise, like, it's prettyetty different from the iPad app.

671
00:52:51,040 --> 00:52:58,160
I don't want to be dramatic and say it's it's rethought from the ground up necessarily, but, um, like you can arrow key between tasks, for example.

672
00:52:58,960 --> 00:53:03,600
Um, and then you hit enter to like expand your task details and edit those details.

673
00:53:04,080 --> 00:53:09,680
Um, so that means that there's like this concept of a task being selected but not actually open.

674
00:53:10,080 --> 00:53:18,160
And there was not that concept on iPhone where you just tap a task and it's immediately, uh, interactive and you're seeing its details and stuff like that.

675
00:53:18,320 --> 00:53:20,720
So it was kind of like this intermediate state that I had to add.

676
00:53:21,360 --> 00:53:30,320
Um, so there were a lot of little things like that that even I didn't.t even think about but just kind of went into, you know, that doesn't really work how I'd expected it to work on the Mac.

677
00:53:30,640 --> 00:53:32,240
And so let me go back and rethink that.

678
00:53:32,480 --> 00:53:38,480
And if I was starting fresh, opening stuff for the first time, what would I expect to happen in this scenario?

679
00:53:39,040 --> 00:53:46,000
Because it's difficult not to let other versions of the app like iPhone and iPad paint your expectations of Matt.

680
00:53:46,960 --> 00:53:49,360
You don't want it to just be a big iPad app, for example.

681
00:53:50,320 --> 00:53:54,480
And so there were a lot of times where I thought, let's start fresh, rethink.

682
00:53:54,640 --> 00:53:55,840
This is a brand new to-do list app.

683
00:53:56,000 --> 00:53:57,360
I just downloaded it from the App Store.

684
00:53:57,840 --> 00:53:59,360
What do I expect to happen in this scenario?

685
00:53:59,600 --> 00:54:01,840
Or how do I expect to edit this or stuff like that.

686
00:54:02,080 --> 00:54:04,080
And then I tried to build it from that aspect.

687
00:54:04,640 --> 00:54:25,280
One of the most interesting things about macOS development whenever I first did it was realizing that one of the biggest differences between building for the iPad and building for the Mac in particular is the opposite of what I would have thought, which is that on the Mac, people want to make things small or be able to make things small.

688
00:54:25,760 --> 00:54:33,600
Like usually, the thinking goes like, okay, well, how do I make this full screen thing not look silly?

689
00:54:34,560 --> 00:54:36,000
But then maybe I'm different.

690
00:54:36,720 --> 00:54:42,480
But like how many people full screen the Apple Notes app or the Reminders app on their Mac?

691
00:54:42,960 --> 00:54:45,040
I bet the vast majority don't.

692
00:54:45,200 --> 00:54:50,240
Now Chrome obviously is full screen, Final Cut Pro, Figma, you know, these are all full screen.

693
00:54:50,640 --> 00:55:02,400
But one of the things for productivity apps in particular that in reality I think most people, or maybe it's just me, I don't know, but it's the ability to go way smaller than you would actually go on the iPad.

694
00:55:02,640 --> 00:55:10,240
And even though the iPad now you can make it smaller, the default experience is full screen and you have to make that good.

695
00:55:10,640 --> 00:55:14,800
And there's even limits on how it can't go nearly as as small as it can on the Mac.

696
00:55:15,120 --> 00:55:18,560
But if you open QuickTime and it's just audio, it's like it's just this little tiny thing.

697
00:55:18,720 --> 00:55:20,240
And that's one of the beauty of the platform.

698
00:55:21,200 --> 00:55:30,160
But it's weird when you, when you've been developing on iOS, you know, for your whole career and you think of this as like, this is the small one.

699
00:55:30,240 --> 00:55:31,200
And then I move over to the big one.

700
00:55:31,280 --> 00:55:33,360
And then you're like, oh, actually I can go smaller here.

701
00:55:33,520 --> 00:55:40,960
If it's like, size is just completely arbitrary here in a way that is not remotely the case on these mobile platforms.

702
00:55:41,360 --> 00:55:42,080
That's so true.

703
00:55:42,240 --> 00:55:42,320
Yeah.

704
00:55:42,560 --> 00:55:44,480
And it's true in terms of like tap areas too.

705
00:55:44,560 --> 00:55:52,560
Like on iPad, you just have to have big buttons for fingers and on Mac, yeah, you can be specific down to the pixel, which is pretty cool.

706
00:55:53,040 --> 00:56:02,880
But I mean, like, the other cool thing you can do is like with the multi window support is you can have like these little pockets of information throughout your screen or like pinned to the top of your windows.

707
00:56:03,120 --> 00:56:10,640
So you can, you can make stuff windows pretty dang small to the point where like, I don't really know who would use this, but like, it's kinda cool.

708
00:56:10,800 --> 00:56:16,160
Like, you could just have your monthly goals sitting up, for example, uh, just in like a tiny box in the corner of the screen.

709
00:56:16,240 --> 00:56:23,680
So yeah, there's a lot of affordances like that on Mac that just make productivity apps in particular very fun to design for.

710
00:56:24,000 --> 00:56:25,360
Yeah, I totally agree.

711
00:56:26,000 --> 00:56:29,600
And then, uh, before we like wrap off, how did the launch of the Mac go?

712
00:56:29,840 --> 00:56:32,000
Like, did you make a big ado out of it?

713
00:56:32,240 --> 00:56:34,480
Did you see much impact on the business from it?

714
00:56:35,200 --> 00:56:44,080
Yeah, um, on the learning side, uh, the Mac app took way longer to review and approve than I expected.

715
00:56:44,400 --> 00:56:56,160
So for anyone making a Mac app, I pushed it onto the App Store for review a week before I planned to launch, which I, which I thought was really generous just to give plenty of time if they, you know, have any problems or anything, I can work them out.

716
00:56:56,720 --> 00:56:58,720
But I ended up expediting that review.

717
00:57:00,240 --> 00:57:04,000
Then like contacted them and doing everything I could to get it out as soon as possible.

718
00:57:04,320 --> 00:57:05,200
I remember seeing this on Twitter.

719
00:57:05,360 --> 00:57:06,800
Yeah, because you kept being like, Yeah.

720
00:57:07,040 --> 00:57:08,480
Well, I guess it's not happening today.

721
00:57:09,200 --> 00:57:09,200
Yeah.

722
00:57:10,240 --> 00:57:16,800
So I ended up launching three days after I wanted to launch, which wasn't ideal because I put some marketing into that launch.

723
00:57:17,200 --> 00:57:22,640
Next time, I would give it honestly probably three weeks just to give you plenty of time to weed it.

724
00:57:22,960 --> 00:57:27,360
I think I've had a similar experience where sometimes the Mac can take way longer for reasons I don't understand.

725
00:57:27,680 --> 00:57:34,640
Whenever you have a new version on test flight and you have to get that one reviewed, that one can take way longer than even normal app review on the Mac for some reason.

726
00:57:34,960 --> 00:57:36,480
But the other thing is.

727
00:57:37,600 --> 00:57:45,280
in the period we're in now with all the vibe coding apps clogging up app reviews, everything is going through weird periods.

728
00:57:45,840 --> 00:57:56,880
I won't say everything is going slow because I've heard from a lot of people that are like, oh yeah, I started a new app and or submitted a new app and it got reviewed in 24 hours, which can sometimes happen.

729
00:57:57,440 --> 00:58:09,600
But it definitely is very often the case where it seems like things get really clogged up and people will sit and review for a week or more even on iOS for a like standard app that doesn't have weird entitlements or anything like that.

730
00:58:10,000 --> 00:58:10,000
Yes.

731
00:58:10,480 --> 00:58:10,560
Yeah.

732
00:58:12,000 --> 00:58:15,600
I projected for a lot of different things over the Mac app, which is part of the reason it took so long.

733
00:58:15,840 --> 00:58:17,840
But all of those things were not new to the Mac app.

734
00:58:18,160 --> 00:58:20,560
They were all things that were already present on the iOS app.

735
00:58:20,880 --> 00:58:23,360
And so it was just me saying, hey, this is already approved.

736
00:58:23,520 --> 00:58:24,560
We've already talked about this.

737
00:58:24,880 --> 00:58:26,800
And so it's just a lot of back and forth with that.

738
00:58:27,360 --> 00:58:35,280
Other than that, I think that the Mac app launch has gone well and I've so far got a lot of good feedback, which has been really exciting for me.

739
00:58:35,440 --> 00:58:36,560
I did have a test flight out.

740
00:58:36,880 --> 00:58:45,200
And like when you launch a new big update or a new on a new platform, you always worry about the bugs and crashes and the things that you just haven't found yet.

741
00:58:45,920 --> 00:58:48,240
And there have been one or two of those things.

742
00:58:48,240 --> 00:58:50,240
Those things, but they weren't even big deals.

743
00:58:50,960 --> 00:58:53,200
And I was able to just resolve it in another update.

744
00:58:53,600 --> 00:58:54,800
It was really awesome.

745
00:58:54,880 --> 00:58:59,840
And it was really satisfying for me because it's NACAP is something that I've been working on for about a year.

746
00:59:00,720 --> 00:59:08,160
And so to have it launched smoothly and to not have like a day one patch that I'm rushing to get out, uh, was nice and relaxing.

747
00:59:08,560 --> 00:59:09,600
So it, it was great.

748
00:59:09,920 --> 00:59:12,240
I'm very happy with it and excited about the future of it.

749
00:59:12,800 --> 00:59:13,120
Awesome.

750
00:59:13,840 --> 00:59:14,160
All right.

751
00:59:14,320 --> 00:59:24,240
Well, on that note, um, I'll ask you the question I ask everyone in the show, which is what's a person or people out there that have inspired you and your work that you would recommend others to check out?

752
00:59:24,480 --> 00:59:24,560
Yeah.

753
00:59:24,720 --> 00:59:26,080
Yeah, I was prepared for this question.

754
00:59:26,240 --> 00:59:30,080
So I've got like a few different names that I think people should definitely check out.

755
00:59:30,960 --> 00:59:37,040
I've got some smaller creators that I think would be worth checking out, but then also some bigger names.

756
00:59:37,600 --> 00:59:43,120
So first one is a bigger one, Capano, I think his name is Steph Ango.

757
00:59:43,440 --> 00:59:44,640
He's founder of Obsidian.

758
00:59:45,680 --> 00:59:48,320
His handle is KEPANO.

759
00:59:49,040 --> 00:59:52,480
Obsidian, if you don't know, is a great note taking app.

760
00:59:53,600 --> 00:59:59,920
And it's, I think that it's the future of software in a lot of ways because it kind of ditches everything proprietary and just keeps it.

761
01:00:00,160 --> 01:00:03,760
gives it into your hands and into the hands of hyper, hyper, hyper customizable.

762
01:00:04,800 --> 01:00:06,960
Yes, yeah, and it is awesome.

763
01:00:07,360 --> 01:00:10,560
It kind of has a big learning curve, but it's it's awesome.

764
01:00:10,640 --> 01:00:12,640
Is that your notebook choice?

765
01:00:12,800 --> 01:00:13,120
Follow them.

766
01:00:13,360 --> 01:00:13,520
It is.

767
01:00:14,000 --> 01:00:15,360
Are you in the what is it called?

768
01:00:16,000 --> 01:00:18,160
The like second brain thing that they do.

769
01:00:18,800 --> 01:00:29,520
I remember when Obsidian was first coming out, it was it was more around this idea of like notes are actually cards and they're all mapped together because it's like it was like this big system.

770
01:00:29,920 --> 01:00:36,720
And I feel like since then it's kind of become the like, uber customizable markdown editor note taking app.

771
01:00:37,680 --> 01:00:42,560
But are you are you all in it on the sort of this is how you map your brain or whatever thing?

772
01:00:42,960 --> 01:00:45,920
Yeah, Second Brain is like a popular Thiago Forte.

773
01:00:46,240 --> 01:00:48,480
Thiago Forte, I think, coined that.

774
01:00:48,640 --> 01:00:51,360
He has a book called something about Second Brain.

775
01:00:51,600 --> 01:00:54,320
But yeah, so I've got 11,000 apps in Obsidian.

776
01:00:54,480 --> 01:00:55,520
Obsidian is awesome.

777
01:00:55,920 --> 01:01:05,040
And the founder, the reason I recommend following him is because he posts a lot of good philosophical and theoretical content about the future of software that I really did.

778
01:01:05,600 --> 01:01:07,120
So it's a great follow.

779
01:01:07,680 --> 01:01:21,840
That's interesting because I think of Obsidian as very different than stuff, where stuff is kind of this hyper native, very opinionated, like expression of a developer.

780
01:01:22,320 --> 01:01:31,280
Whereas Obsidian is like a Home Depot filled with tools that you can use to build, you know, we have, here's lumber, and here's concrete, and here's all this stuff.

781
01:01:31,440 --> 01:01:35,120
You can build whatever you want with it, but like, you need to learn how to do that.

782
01:01:35,520 --> 01:01:37,440
It's a very, very different sort of philosophy.

783
01:01:37,600 --> 01:01:42,160
So I'm curious, why do you think that's the future of software versus the way you have approached software?

784
01:01:42,720 --> 01:01:52,560
One of the best parts of Obsidian, in my perspective, my opinion, is the way that the database is set up where it's all native files on your device.

785
01:01:52,800 --> 01:01:54,000
So there's no cloud storing these.

786
01:01:54,160 --> 01:01:55,920
You can figure out your own cloud solution.

787
01:01:56,080 --> 01:02:02,560
You can let them host it if you want, but there's no proprietary to Obsidian that's keeping you locked in.

788
01:02:03,440 --> 01:02:25,760
And the other great thing about that is that you can navigate to your file directory in an agent like Cloud or Codex and just ask it questions and it can read all these lines of text across all these files and it just loves that environment that it's in and it can answer all kinds of questions for you and do stuff for you without having to go through Obsidian's proprietary APIs.

789
01:02:26,320 --> 01:02:40,640
And so although stuff is not built that way at all right now, that is, it's very difficult to build something like that and also have it be seamless for like, like I mentioned, someone like my grandma who I want to be able to pick up the app and just start creating tasks and checking them off.

790
01:02:41,120 --> 01:02:45,280
There's a lot that goes into making an app like Obsidian, but also that simple.

791
01:02:47,200 --> 01:02:48,320
And so there's that.

792
01:02:48,640 --> 01:02:50,160
